<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sainte-Marie de Gannentaha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1656">1656</a>–<a href="/years/1658">1658</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sainte-Marie de Gannentaha was a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> mission and settlement built in 1656 on the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-lake">Onondaga Lake</a>, near present-day Syracuse, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, at the invitation of the Onondaga. About fifty French colonists and Jesuits lived there, but fearing an attack they secretly abandoned it in March 1658. A reconstruction, Sainte Marie among the Iroquois, now stands near the site.</p></div>
